phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:68:Tidy3D EM feature surface identified from the local codebase and schemas: Simulation plus Scene and Structure containers; geometry families including Box, Sphere, Cylinder, PolySlab, GeometryGroup, Transformed, ClipOperation, GeometryArray, and TriangleMesh-related paths; material families including Medium, PECMedium, PMCMedium, custom and dispersive isotropic media, PoleResidue, Sellmeier, Lorentz, Drude, Debye, LossyMetalMedium, AnisotropicMedium, FullyAnisotropicMedium, CustomAnisotropicMedium, PerturbationMedium, and Medium2D; boundary families including Periodic, PECBoundary, PMCBoundary, BlochBoundary, PML, StablePML, Absorber, ABCBoundary, ModeABCBoundary, Boundary, and BoundarySpec; source families including GaussianPulse, ContinuousWave, CustomSourceTime, BroadbandPulse, UniformCurrentSource, PointDipole, CustomCurrentSource, CustomFieldSource, ModeSource, PlaneWave, GaussianBeam, AstigmaticGaussianBeam, and TFSF; monitor families including FieldMonitor, FieldTimeMonitor, AuxFieldTimeMonitor, MediumMonitor, PermittivityMonitor, FluxMonitor, FluxTimeMonitor, ModeMonitor, ModeSolverMonitor, GaussianOverlapMonitor, AstigmaticGaussianOverlapMonitor, FieldProjectionAngleMonitor, DirectivityMonitor, FieldProjectionCartesianMonitor, FieldProjectionKSpaceMonitor, DiffractionMonitor, SurfaceFieldMonitor, and SurfaceFieldTimeMonitor; and grid or subpixel controls including UniformGrid, CustomGridBoundaries, CustomGrid, AutoGrid, QuasiUniformGrid, GridRefinement, LayerRefinementSpec, GridSpec, ModeSpec, and SubpixelSpec with staircasing and averaging variants. High-value references: `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/simulation.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/structure.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/geometry/base.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/geometry/primitives.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/geometry/polyslab.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/source/current.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/source/field.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/source/time.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/monitor.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/medium.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/boundary.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/grid/grid_spec.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/mode_spec.py`, `../tidy3d/tidy3d/components/subpixel_spec.py`, and `../tidy3d/schemas/Simulation.json`.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:80:Warp backend findings: Warp kernels are Python-authored and JIT-compiled per module; all kernels in a module compile together and should be stabilized early to avoid reload churn. Arrays, vectors, matrices, and structs are strong fits for field buffers and metadata. CUDA launches are asynchronous; streams and events are first-class; graph capture and replay are realistic optimization targets for timestep loops; tile APIs are promising for later shared-memory optimizations but should not block a first Phase 1 backend; and built-in profiling via ScopedTimer, NVTX, and CUDA activity summaries is good enough for Phase 1 instrumentation. Benchmarking guidance from `papers/gpu_benchmarking.pdf` adds that FDTD should be tracked primarily in cells updated per second or Gcells/s, usually as a bandwidth-bound workload, and that benchmark metadata must preserve grid shape, total cells, timestep count, precision, boundary or material class, single-versus-multi-GPU context, and separate end-to-end versus steady-state timing.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:84:Scheduling findings from `papers/systolic_fdtd.pdf`: a naive GPU FDTD is constrained mostly by memory traffic and synchronization, so the important architectural seam is the split between chunk-local interior work, lower-dimensional boundary exchange, and a pluggable execution policy that may later delay or overlap neighbor consumption. Phase 1 should remain bulk-synchronous by default, but chunk metadata and runtime orchestration should already preserve per-face, per-stage halo semantics, interior-versus-boundary planning, and scheduler hooks so future systolic or overlapped execution can be added without redesign.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:130:- `src/autofdtd/core/validation.py` now centralizes Phase 1 normalization rules, unsupported-feature checks, primitive geometry sanity checks, simulation-bound checks, and zero-dimension boundary coercion.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:141:- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `GridSpec` into typed grid IR with per-axis spec payloads, layer-refinement metadata, and resolved boundary and shape metadata, so later compiler work can consume inspectable discretization state instead of reparsing public models.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:161:- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now provides the first typed non-absorbing boundary surface with `Periodic`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, `Boundary`, and `BoundarySpec`, including periodic-opposite-conductor coercion.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:162:- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers the supported boundary subset into typed `PeriodicIR`, `PECBoundaryIR`, `PMCBoundaryIR`, `BoundaryIR`, and `BoundarySpecIR` payloads while leaving unsupported boundary families in the generic boundary envelope.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:167:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now prepare polynomial PML coefficients (`sigma`, `kappa`, `alpha`, attenuation and memory terms), allocate per-face persistent boundary-layer state, and run explicit `electric_pml` / `magnetic_pml` stages after halo handling.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:200:- Phase 1 boundary runtime support now covers `Periodic`, `BlochBoundary`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, and baseline `PML`; `StablePML`, `Absorber`, and ABC-family entries remain outside the runtime compiler until later boundary tasks land.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:202:- Keep Phase 1 PML deliberately narrow: use Tidy3D-shaped `num_layers + parameters + extrude_structures` public models, compile one profile per boundary face, and treat the first runnable backend as a staged damping layer with persistent face memory rather than a full split-field CPML implementation.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:211:- Later geometry, source, monitor, boundary, and grid tasks should route new object models through `autofdtd.core.validation.normalize_component()` or extend its equivalents instead of scattering policy checks across each family.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:219:- Later absorbing-boundary tasks should extend `CompiledBoundarySpec` and the boundary ghost-update stage rather than reworking the public `Boundary` or `BoundarySpec` container semantics; Bloch, symmetry, and baseline PML metadata now already live on that runtime contract.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:220:- `task-022` should either preserve the `PMLParams` coefficient-shape contract for `StablePML` / `Absorber` or add a clearly separate compiled-face payload; `allocate_pml_boundary_state()` and `apply_boundary_stages()` are the current extension seams.
phase1/runs/task-023-attempt-001/codex.stdout.txt:221:- Later ABC-boundary work should treat the current `pml_faces` metadata on `CompiledBoundarySpec` as the legacy absorbing-face alias and prefer the newer `stable_pml_faces` / `absorber_faces` tags when family-specific behavior matters.
