phase1/working-memory.md:44:- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` into typed IR families while runtime compilation still targets the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.
phase1/working-memory.md:47:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` by first reducing them to equivalent `PoleResidue` terms, re-tagging the compiled coefficients with the public family name, and failing explicitly when converted poles are too stiff for the current exact-hold timestep path.
phase1/working-memory.md:49:- `src/autofdtd/materials/anisotropic.py` now provides `AnisotropicMedium` as the implemented diagonal tensor family plus explicit `FullyAnisotropicMedium` and `Medium2D` defer-policy surfaces; `Medium2D` is parseable and convertible to volumetric `AnisotropicMedium`, but direct simulation use stays rejected in Phase 1.
phase1/working-memory.md:50:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `AnisotropicMedium` into `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` with one scalar constitutive branch per principal axis, allowing nondispersive, dispersive, and clamp-mode components to coexist without introducing a dense-tensor runtime yet.
phase1/working-memory.md:55:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile the supported subset into explicit halo metadata and apply periodic-copy or conductor-reflection ghost-cell updates for `(nx, ny, nz, 3)` vector fields.
phase1/working-memory.md:56:- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now implements `BlochBoundary` plus `Boundary.bloch(...)`; runtime compilation lowers Bloch axes into explicit conjugate phase factors on `minus` faces and forward phase factors on `plus` faces, which forces complex field storage even when `bloch_vec == 0`.
phase1/working-memory.md:59:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now prepare polynomial PML coefficients (`sigma`, `kappa`, `alpha`, attenuation and memory terms), allocate per-face persistent boundary-layer state, and run explicit `electric_pml` / `magnetic_pml` stages after halo handling.
phase1/working-memory.md:64:- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile `ABCBoundary` into explicit first-order recurrence coefficients plus persistent per-face ABC state, while `ModeABCBoundary` fails with a clear runtime-deferred error until mode-solver work lands.
phase1/working-memory.md:69:- Reserve subsystem namespaces now: `api`, `core`, `geometry`, `materials`, `boundaries`, `sources`, `monitors`, `grid`, `modes`, `ir`, `compiler`, `runtime`, `kernels`, `diagnostics`, `examples`.
phase1/working-memory.md:70:- Use `hatchling` with a `src/` layout and ship a small CLI (`autofdtd-feature-matrix`) so the scaffold has a concrete install/runtime smoke target before solver code exists.
phase1/working-memory.md:80:- Keep `GeometryArray` as a public wrapper only; repeated placements lower into ordinary grouped and transformed instances so runtime/compiler code can treat arrays as pre-expanded ordered geometry.
phase1/working-memory.md:87:- Treat PEC and PMC as explicit constitutive clamp modes in coefficient compilation and runtime helpers instead of pretending they are extremely lossy ordinary media.
phase1/working-memory.md:89:- Phase 1 PoleResidue runtime support is electric-only and uses one complex polarization accumulator per pole; magnetic dispersion remains out of scope until later material families require it.
phase1/working-memory.md:93:- Phase 1 anisotropy stops at diagonal tensor media: `AnisotropicMedium` is implemented and IR-lowered, `FullyAnisotropicMedium` remains an explicit deferred error for runtime compilation, and `Medium2D` remains a deferred direct-simulation feature with offline conversion helpers only.
phase1/working-memory.md:95:- Phase 1 boundary runtime support now covers `Periodic`, `BlochBoundary`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, and baseline `PML`; `StablePML`, `Absorber`, and ABC-family entries remain outside the runtime compiler until later boundary tasks land.
phase1/working-memory.md:98:- Keep `StablePML` as a preset-driven extension of the baseline staged PML backend in Phase 1 rather than a separate field-splitting formulation; runtime differentiation is through compiled profile metadata and defaults, not a distinct kernel family.
phase1/working-memory.md:112:- Later runtime and chunk-planning tasks can treat `IsotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus `ConstitutiveMode` as the stable baseline constitutive contract before dispersive and anisotropic state is added.
phase1/working-memory.md:113:- Later Lorentz, Drude, Debye, and Sellmeier tasks should lower onto the `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` auxiliary-state contract whenever the family can be reduced to pole-residue terms instead of creating parallel runtime state layouts.
phase1/working-memory.md:114:- Later subpixel materialization work can target `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus the diagonal anisotropic kernel path instead of inventing a separate tensor-material runtime for effective-media smoothing.
phase1/working-memory.md:115:- Later absorbing-boundary tasks should extend `CompiledBoundarySpec` and the boundary ghost-update stage rather than reworking the public `Boundary` or `BoundarySpec` container semantics; Bloch, symmetry, and baseline PML metadata now already live on that runtime contract.
phase1/loop.prompt.md:5:Drive Phase 1 of autofdtd from an almost-empty repository to a credible, pip-installable, GPU-first FDTD package whose feature plan is anchored explicitly to the local Tidy3D EM API surface. The loop must produce a Tidy3D-inspired public API, a tagged execution IR, a scene-compilation pipeline, a chunk-based multi-node-ready runtime, Warp kernel conventions, concrete implementations for the highest-priority Tidy3D feature families, validation examples, and documentation without copying protected code or docs from any reference project.
phase1/loop.prompt.md:32:- Separate scene modeling, scene compilation, runtime stepping, and IO or packaging into distinct subsystems.
phase1/loop.prompt.md:72:Scheduling findings from `papers/systolic_fdtd.pdf`: a naive GPU FDTD is constrained mostly by memory traffic and synchronization, so the important architectural seam is the split between chunk-local interior work, lower-dimensional boundary exchange, and a pluggable execution policy that may later delay or overlap neighbor consumption. Phase 1 should remain bulk-synchronous by default, but chunk metadata and runtime orchestration should already preserve per-face, per-stage halo semantics, interior-versus-boundary planning, and scheduler hooks so future systolic or overlapped execution can be added without redesign.
phase1/loop.prompt.md:80:Working-memory seed facts that later tasks should preserve: chunk is the architectural unit while rank or device is placement; stored topology and logical topology diverge because of symmetry and periodic or Bloch remapping; scene compilation must remain distinct from stepping; continuous problem specifications must lower through explicit interpolation and restriction into discrete execution artifacts; geometry lowering and materialization must precede serious kernels; subpixel smoothing can induce effective anisotropy even for physically isotropic materials; stage-specific halos and monitor metadata should be explicit; Tidy3D-style compatibility should be represented as a tagged immutable IR with normalization rules and unsupported-feature policy; runtime metrics should include Gcells/s plus separate end-to-end and steady-state timing; and near-to-far should be built on top of DFT monitor infrastructure rather than as an isolated feature.
phase1/loop.prompt.md:92:  - logging and convergence behavior are first-class runtime deliverables, not incidental byproducts
phase1/loop.prompt.md:93:  - kernel/runtime support must be explicit for materials, boundaries, sources, monitors, and postprocessing rather than implied by API support
