Support FieldProjectionAngleMonitor, FieldProjectionCartesianMonitor, FieldProjectionKSpaceMonitor, and DirectivityMonitor or a documented Phase 1 subset with clear error behavior and explicit projection-kernel support.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Projection monitors have many edge cases, so keep the task focused and explicit.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-043 - Implement DiffractionMonitor and diffraction-specific limitations\nSuccess: Provide DiffractionMonitor support or a narrow supported subset with explicit validation for unsupported order-count or medium constraints and the corresponding diffraction-kernel support.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Diffraction is important enough to split out from the other projection monitors.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-044 - Implement surface-field monitor families\nSuccess: Support SurfaceFieldMonitor and SurfaceFieldTimeMonitor or a documented subset with explicit runtime behavior, surface-sampling kernels, and tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Surface monitors are useful both directly and as building blocks for postprocessing.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-045 - Build the scene-compilation pipeline from public API and IR into compiled runtime artifacts\nSuccess: Document and begin implementing a compiler-style pipeline that lowers validated public API objects into IR and then into coefficient fields, source plans, monitor plans, chunk metadata, and runtime arrays.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task ties the feature-specific surfaces into an executable spine.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-046 - Implement geometry discretization, interpolation, and subpixel materialization\nSuccess: Build the geometry-to-grid compilation path that handles precedence, interpolation or restriction concerns, coefficient sampling, and a scoped first pass at subpixel smoothing with tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This is where the geometry and material feature families become runtime data. Second-order subpixel smoothing depends on the anisotropic material path already existing.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-047 - Define the chunk contract and multi-node decomposition architecture\nSuccess: Land an architecture note that defines what a chunk owns, how chunks expose halo and monitor metadata, how chunk placement maps onto devices or nodes, and how this avoids blocking later phases.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Base this on the Meep findings that chunk is the core architectural unit rather than rank.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-048 - Define Warp backend conventions and instrumentation strategy\nSuccess: Document and implement the initial Warp backend conventions for module organization, dtype or device policy, persistent arrays, capture-safe stepping, and profiling hooks.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task establishes backend guardrails before the heavy kernels land.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-049 - Implement vacuum Yee update kernels and the capture-safe step skeleton\nSuccess: Document and implement the explicit vacuum E and H update kernels, field staggering rules, and capture-safe step skeleton with clear launch ordering and representative tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task isolates the core timestepping spine before material, boundary, and source complications are layered on.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-050 - Implement constitutive and material-update kernel stages\nSuccess: Provide the constitutive and material-update kernel stages needed for isotropic, dispersive, and anisotropic materials that Phase 1 claims to support, with clear staging and tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task ties the material feature tasks into the runtime in an explicit way.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-051 - Implement boundary and absorbing-layer kernel stages\nSuccess: Provide the explicit runtime or kernel stages needed for conductor boundaries, periodic or Bloch remapping, and absorbing-layer updates claimed by Phase 1.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Keep boundary runtime support separate from source and material kernel work.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-052 - Implement source-injection kernel stages and source scheduling\nSuccess: Provide the explicit kernel or runtime stages for current-source, field-source, beam, plane-wave, and TFSF injection claimed by Phase 1, with clear scheduling semantics and tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task keeps source runtime support visible instead of scattering it across API-only tasks.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-053 - Implement monitor accumulation, projection, and extraction kernel stages\nSuccess: Provide the explicit runtime or kernel stages needed for field, flux, mode, projection, diffraction, and surface-monitor accumulation and extraction claimed by Phase 1.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task makes the monitor runtime spine concrete instead of assuming monitor API support implies executable support.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-054 - Implement chunk-aware runtime orchestration and staged halo exchange\nSuccess: Create runtime components that partition compiled scenes into chunks, manage stage-specific halo metadata, and provide the halo pack, unpack, and exchange kernel support needed for chunk-aware control flow.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Keep this bulk-synchronous and credible first; overlap and advanced scheduling can stay as later extensions.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-055 - Wire the public API to normalization, IR lowering, and execution entrypoints\nSuccess: Users can define nontrivial Tidy3D-style simulations, have them normalized and lowered into the IR, inspect or serialize that IR, and execute them through the same backend entrypoints.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task should prove that execution is driven by the IR rather than the original Python object graph.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-056 - Implement runtime metrics, benchmark helpers, and execution evidence capture\nSuccess: Execution paths emit initialization time, JIT time, steady-state timestep metrics, Gcells-per-second style metrics, and other key runtime evidence in a structured and testable form.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task should align with the Warp instrumentation strategy and the GPU benchmarking paper.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-057 - Implement near-to-far and related postprocessing on top of the monitor infrastructure\nSuccess: Provide an initial near-to-far workflow and other key postprocessing routines that build on the DFT monitor foundation and include the postprocessing-kernel support they require, backed by examples or tests.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: Do not implement near-to-far as a disconnected special case.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-058 - Create canonical validation examples and feature-integration tests\nSuccess: Land runnable validation examples and automated tests that exercise the implemented Phase 1 feature families, including vacuum propagation, dielectric slab or waveguide, PML boundaries, symmetry-reduced behavior, source injection, monitor recording, mode-source or mode-monitor workflows, convergence or shutoff behavior, and near-to-far or diffraction-related workflows where supported.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: These examples should become the evidence base for both feature coverage and later validation work.\n\n## [QUEUED] task-059 - Finish README, feature coverage docs, unsupported-feature policy, and Phase 1 readiness summary\nSuccess: Document architecture, current feature coverage against the Tidy3D matrix, unsupported-feature behavior, developer workflows, and the evidence that Phase 1 is ready to hand into deeper validation and optimization phases.\nAttempts: 0\nLast Run: -\nLast Duration: -\nCompleted At: -\nNotes: This task should consolidate the Phase 1 evidence base rather than inventing new architecture.\n","numLines":478,"startLine":1,"totalLines":478}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_function_a15fxht0aave_2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Phase 1 Maxwell Solver Loop Working Memory\n2\t\n3\tUse this file for cumulative findings, reusable facts, decisions, and cross-task notes.\n4\t\n5\tKeep it concise. Do not repeat information already captured in `loop.prompt.md` or other local files.\n6\tPrefer referencing local file paths and distilled takeaways instead of copying long excerpts.\n7\t\n8\tSuggested sections:\n9\t\n10\t- Facts\n11\t- Decisions\n12\t- Open Questions\n13\t- Reusable References\n14\t- Task Handoffs\n15\t\n16\t## Facts\n17\t\n18\t- `task-001` starts from an effectively empty repo with no `src/` package or tooling.\n19\t- `phase1/feature-checklist.md` is now mirrored in code via `src/autofdtd/planning.py` so later tasks can reuse a local feature/status source instead of reparsing markdown.\n20\t- `src/autofdtd/core/containers.py` now holds the first frozen tagged container models for `Structure`, `Scene`, and `Simulation`; `src/autofdtd/api/simulation.py` re-exports them as the public API surface.\n21\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now defines the first versioned execution IR, with `ComponentIR`, `StructureIR`, `SceneIR`, `SimulationIR`, and `ExecutionPackageIR` as the transport contract.\n22\t- `src/autofdtd/core/validation.py` now centralizes Phase 1 normalization rules, unsupported-feature checks, primitive geometry sanity checks, simulation-bound checks, and zero-dimension boundary coercion.\n23\t- `src/autofdtd/core/models.py` now provides the shared frozen model contract with `model_tag()`, `copy_update()`, `json_ready()`, and stable JSON serialization helpers reused by both public containers and IR models.\n24\t- `src/autofdtd/geometry/primitives.py` now provides typed `Box`, `Sphere`, and `Cylinder` public models plus `GeometryTransform`, bounds, point-containment, and translation helpers.\n25\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers primitive geometries into typed `BoxIR`, `SphereIR`, and `CylinderIR` payloads with explicit bounds and scene-compilation transform metadata.\n26\t- `src/autofdtd/geometry/polyslab.py` now provides a typed `PolySlab` model for simple-polygon extrusions with bounds, containment, translation, and simple-polygon validation; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers it into `PolySlabIR`.\n27\t- `src/autofdtd/geometry/composite.py` now provides `GeometryGroup`, `Transformed`, `GeometryArray`, and a narrow `ClipOperation` subset (`union`, `intersection`, `difference`) for Phase 1 scene construction.\n28\t- `GeometryTransform` now supports explicit rigid-transform composition plus translation, rotation, reflection, and conservative transformed-bounds evaluation for wrapper lowering and scene queries.\n29\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now carries typed wrapper IR via `GeometryGroupIR`, `TransformedIR`, and `ClipOperationIR`.\n30\t- `GeometryArray` uses Tidy3D-style `geometry + offsets + transforms` public semantics, but Phase 1 keeps transforms rigid and linear-only and lowers arrays into explicit grouped instances for IR and overlap evaluation.\n31\t- `Scene.structure_precedence()` and `Scene.resolve_structure_stack_at_point()` now expose concrete overlap order with `source_index`, `resolved_priority`, and `precedence_rank`, and `StructureIR` carries the same metadata for later compiler stages.\n32\t- `src/autofdtd/grid/specs.py` now provides `AutoGrid`, `GridRefinement`, and `LayerRefinementSpec` in addition to the manual grid models; the current AutoGrid path is wavelength-driven, axis-local, and only applies axis-aligned layer refinement metadata.\n33\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `GridSpec` into typed grid IR with per-axis spec payloads, layer-refinement metadata, and resolved boundary and shape metadata, so later compiler work can consume inspectable discretization state instead of reparsing public models.\n34\t- `src/autofdtd/grid/subpixel.py` now provides the first explicit subpixel policy layer with `SubpixelSpec`, `Staircasing`, and `PolarizedAveraging`, plus Tidy3D-style `bool` coercion for `Simulation.subpixel`.\n35\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers subpixel settings into `SubpixelSpecIR` with explicit averaging-vs-staircasing targets and an anisotropic-materialization flag for later scene compilation.\n36\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/isotropic.py` now provides typed `Medium`, `PECMedium`, and `PMCMedium` public models with validation and normalization helpers; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers them into typed `MediumIR`, `PECMediumIR`, and `PMCMediumIR`.\n37\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles isotropic constitutive coefficients, exposes scene-point medium sampling, and keeps explicit clamp-zero modes for `PECMedium` and `PMCMedium`.\n38\t- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now provides the first staged constitutive-update helpers with a NumPy path and optional Warp-availability reporting.\n39\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now provides a Phase 1 `PoleResidue` public model with JSON-safe complex-pair storage, causal validation (`Re(a) <= 0`), `eps_model()`, and limited `Medium` conversion helpers.\n40\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now also provides a passive homogeneous `Sellmeier` model with `(B_i, C_i)` coefficient storage, direct `eps_model()` evaluation, single-pole `from_dispersion()` fitting, and explicit lowering to an equivalent `PoleResidue` representation.\n41\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now also provides passive homogeneous `Lorentz`, damped `Drude`, and passive homogeneous `Debye` models with family-specific analytical coefficients and explicit lowering to equivalent `PoleResidue` payloads.\n42\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers supported dispersive media into typed `PoleResidueIR` payloads instead of a generic medium envelope.\n43\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Sellmeier` into a typed `SellmeierIR` while keeping runtime compilation on the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.\n44\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` into typed IR families while runtime compilation still targets the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.\n45\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `PoleResidue` media into explicit epsilon-infinity electric coefficients plus per-pole exact-hold recurrence terms for complex auxiliary polarization state.\n46\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Sellmeier` by first reducing it to an equivalent `PoleResidue`, then re-tagging the compiled coefficients as `medium_type=\"Sellmeier\"` so scene diagnostics preserve the public family name.\n47\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` by first reducing them to equivalent `PoleResidue` terms, re-tagging the compiled coefficients with the public family name, and failing explicitly when converted poles are too stiff for the current exact-hold timestep path.\n48\t- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now allocates explicit PoleResidue auxiliary state and applies a staged `pole_residue_electric_update()` that subtracts a compiled polarization-current estimate from the baseline electric update.\n49\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/anisotropic.py` now provides `AnisotropicMedium` as the implemented diagonal tensor family plus explicit `FullyAnisotropicMedium` and `Medium2D` defer-policy surfaces; `Medium2D` is parseable and convertible to volumetric `AnisotropicMedium`, but direct simulation use stays rejected in Phase 1.\n50\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `AnisotropicMedium` into `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` with one scalar constitutive branch per principal axis, allowing nondispersive, dispersive, and clamp-mode components to coexist without introducing a dense-tensor runtime yet.\n51\t- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now provides `allocate_anisotropic_state()`, `anisotropic_electric_update()`, and `anisotropic_magnetic_update()` for diagonal tensor updates with per-axis dispersive auxiliary state.\n52\t- `src/autofdtd/materials/advanced.py` now maps `LossyMetalMedium`, `PerturbationMedium`, `PerturbationPoleResidue`, `CustomMedium`, and `CustomAnisotropicMedium` into explicit Phase 1 policy surfaces, and `src/autofdtd/core/validation.py` buckets unknown `Custom*` medium tags into the clear custom-media reject path instead of treating them as unmapped types.\n53\t- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now provides the first typed non-absorbing boundary surface with `Periodic`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, `Boundary`, and `BoundarySpec`, including periodic-opposite-conductor coercion.\n54\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers the supported boundary subset into typed `PeriodicIR`, `PECBoundaryIR`, `PMCBoundaryIR`, `BoundaryIR`, and `BoundarySpecIR` payloads while leaving unsupported boundary families in the generic boundary envelope.\n55\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile the supported subset into explicit halo metadata and apply periodic-copy or conductor-reflection ghost-cell updates for `(nx, ny, nz, 3)` vector fields.\n56\t- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now implements `BlochBoundary` plus `Boundary.bloch(...)`; runtime compilation lowers Bloch axes into explicit conjugate phase factors on `minus` faces and forward phase factors on `plus` faces, which forces complex field storage even when `bloch_vec == 0`.\n57\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py` and `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now attach symmetry-axis metadata as explicit electric and magnetic sign transforms derived from mirror parity, so chunk planning and future reduced-domain exchange can reuse the same compiled transform contract as conductor reflections.\n58\t- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now implements baseline `PML` and `PMLParams`; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers them into typed `PMLIR` and `PMLParamsIR`, and `BoundarySpecIR` now records explicit `pml_axes` and `pml_faces`.\n59\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now prepare polynomial PML coefficients (`sigma`, `kappa`, `alpha`, attenuation and memory terms), allocate per-face persistent boundary-layer state, and run explicit `electric_pml` / `magnetic_pml` stages after halo handling.\n60\t- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now also implements `AbsorberParams`, `StablePML`, and `Absorber`, with Tidy3D-aligned default presets, explicit constructors on `Boundary` / `BoundarySpec`, and zero-dimension coercion that treats all absorbing families consistently.\n61\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py` now compiles `StablePML` onto the existing staged PML coefficient path with a separate profile tag and compiles `Absorber` into conductivity-only attenuation coefficients plus a reflective outer-wall policy; `CompiledBoundarySpec` now records `stable_pml_*` and `absorber_*` face metadata in addition to the existing absorbing-face alias.\n62\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `StablePML` and `Absorber` into typed `StablePMLIR` and `AbsorberIR` payloads, with `AbsorberParamsIR` for conductivity-only profile metadata.\n63\t- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now adds `ABCBoundary` as an implemented first-order subset with explicit effective-medium inputs, plus parseable deferred `ModeABCBoundary`, `BroadbandModeABCSpec`, and `BroadbandModeABCFitterParam` compatibility surfaces.\n64\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile `ABCBoundary` into explicit first-order recurrence coefficients plus persistent per-face ABC state, while `ModeABCBoundary` fails with a clear runtime-deferred error until mode-solver work lands.\n65\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `ABCBoundary` into typed `ABCBoundaryIR` and records `abc_axes` / `abc_faces` on `BoundarySpecIR`; `ModeABCBoundary` remains a deferred public-model surface rather than a validated simulation feature.\n66\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/runtime.py` now compiles `Simulation` runtime controls into explicit `CompiledRuntimeControls` metadata with CFL-derived `dt`, Tidy3D-style `num_time_steps`, optional step-count override, shutoff policy, and carried `normalize_index`; `src/autofdtd/runtime/controls.py` evaluates those policies against integrated electric-field intensity without entangling them with the future Maxwell stepper.\n67\t- `src/autofdtd/diagnostics/runtime_logging.py` now provides `RuntimeProgressLogger`, immutable `RuntimeLogEvent` / `RuntimeExecutionLog` records, and explicit `RuntimeConvergenceEvidence` capture; `src/autofdtd/runtime/controls.py` now exposes `run_until_stop_with_logging()` as the logged execution seam for precomputed intensity histories.\n68\t- `src/autofdtd/sources/time.py` now provides Phase 1 `GaussianPulse` and `ContinuousWave` source-time profiles with finite parameter validation, analytic waveform evaluation, frequency-range helpers, and JSON-stable serialization through the shared tagged-model base.\n69\t- `src/autofdtd/sources/time.py` now also implements a supported `BroadbandPulse` Gaussian-subset wrapper and an interpolated `CustomSourceTime` with explicit `(time_samples, envelope_values)` storage using JSON-safe complex pairs.\n70\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers source-time profiles into typed `GaussianPulseIR` and `ContinuousWaveIR` payloads, and generic source component envelopes serialize nested `source_time` entries as typed IR dicts rather than opaque model payloads.\n71\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now also lowers `BroadbandPulse` and `CustomSourceTime` into typed IR payloads carrying derived timing/frequency metadata plus serialized custom-envelope samples.\n72\t- `src/autofdtd/sources/current.py` now implements `UniformCurrentSource` with Tidy3D-aligned `center`, `size`, `polarization`, `source_time`, `interpolate`, `confine_to_bounds`, and `current_amplitude_definition` fields plus explicit `field_kind`, `component_axis`, `zero_size_axes`, `placement_kind`, and `support_bounds` semantics.\n73\t- `src/autofdtd/compiler/sources.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/sources.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/sources.py` now compile uniform current sources onto resolved primal-cell grids using overlap-or-interpolation axis placement, total-current normalization by transverse extent, and staged NumPy injection helpers for `(nx, ny, nz, 3)` electric or magnetic field buffers.\n74\t- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `UniformCurrentSource` into typed `UniformCurrentSourceIR` payloads instead of only the generic source envelope, and `src/autofdtd/examples/current_sources.py` plus `tests/test_sources_current.py` exercise placement, IR lowering, compilation, and runtime injection end to end.\n75\t\n76\t## Decisions\n77\t\n78\t- Reserve subsystem namespaces now: `api`, `core`, `geometry`, `materials`, `boundaries`, `sources`, `monitors`, `grid`, `modes`, `ir`, `compiler`, `runtime`, `kernels`, `diagnostics`, `examples`.\n79\t- Use `hatchling` with a `src/` layout and ship a small CLI (`autofdtd-feature-matrix`) so the scaffold has a concrete install/runtime smoke target before solver code exists.\n80\t- Keep this task focused on packaging, docs, and planning metadata; full tagged models remain for `task-002` and `task-003`.\n81\t- Model ordered overlap explicitly through `Scene.structures_in_resolution_order()`, sorting by resolved priority and then insertion order so later structures still win ties during sequential materialization.\n82\t- Keep the initial container layer serialization-light: `to_payload()`, `to_json_text()`, and `write_json()` are enough for snapshots and handoff, while `task-005` can generalize the shared model base.\n83\t- Use a generic family-tagged component envelope in the IR so Phase 1 can package realistic simulations before every feature family has concrete typed IR classes; later tasks can specialize payloads without changing bundle shape.\n84\t- Bundle packaging is directory-based: `manifest.json` points at a hashed `simulation.json` entrypoint, avoiding self-referential manifest hashing while staying suitable for remote upload or local caching.\n85\t- Core validation is checklist-driven: implemented feature tags pass, deferred or rejected tags fail early with explicit errors, and known primitive geometry payloads validate only when enough fields are present so placeholder tagged dicts can survive until their concrete family tasks land.\n86\t- Shared model behavior should live in `autofdtd.core.models`; public API and IR classes should subclass it rather than duplicating serialization or copy helpers in subsystem-local base classes.\n87\t- Primitive geometry stays axis-aligned in Phase 1 public models; scene-compilation transforms are still explicit so later `Transformed` and grouped-geometry tasks can lower onto the same contract instead of rewriting primitive handling.\n88\t- Structure precedence is now inspectable with `Scene.resolve_structure_at_point()`, which mirrors materialization order by applying primitive containment checks in resolved-priority order.\n89\t- Keep `GeometryArray` as a public wrapper only; repeated placements lower into ordinary grouped and transformed instances so runtime/compiler code can treat arrays as pre-expanded ordered geometry.\n90\t- Phase 1 `PolySlab` scope is intentionally limited to constant-cross-section extrusions of simple polygons on principal axes; non-zero `dilation` or `sidewall_angle`, self-intersections, and curved or boolean polygon features must fail explicitly until later geometry tasks land.\n91\t- Keep the feature ledger conservative for clipping: `ClipOperation` remains a deferred family in `planning.py`, but geometry validation explicitly allows the implemented subset and rejects unsupported boolean ops with clear errors.\n92\t- Phase 1 transform wrappers are rigid only; free-form affine matrices and exact boolean meshing remain out of scope even though the public model now supports grouped and transformed composition.\n93\t- Keep the top-level simulation grid surface strict: `grid_spec` must be a `GridSpec`, `AutoGrid` requires `GridSpec.wavelength`, and `QuasiUniformGrid` remains an explicit deferred error.\n94\t- Phase 1 AutoGrid is deliberately narrow: derive a vacuum baseline from wavelength and domain size, discretize piecewise with a simple `max_scale` grading pass, and limit refinement inputs to `LayerRefinementSpec` body and bound hints rather than full geometry-driven meshing.\n95\t- Keep Phase 1 subpixel support narrow and explicit: dielectric interfaces may use `PolarizedAveraging` or `Staircasing`, while metal, PEC, PMC, and lossy-metal interfaces stay staircasing-only until anisotropic media and conformal policies land.\n96\t- Treat PEC and PMC as explicit constitutive clamp modes in coefficient compilation and runtime helpers instead of pretending they are extremely lossy ordinary media.\n97\t- Phase 1 `PoleResidue` keeps complex numbers JSON-safe as `(real, imag)` tuples in the public API and IR so serialization stays stable without inventing ad hoc string encoding.\n98\t- Phase 1 PoleResidue runtime support is electric-only and uses one complex polarization accumulator per pole; magnetic dispersion remains out of scope until later material families require it.\n99\t- Phase 1 `Sellmeier` support is intentionally passive-only and homogeneous-only: `B_i >= 0`, `C_i >= 0`, zero-`C_i` terms fold into `eps_inf`, and all timestep execution reuses the existing electric-only `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` path instead of creating a new auxiliary-state layout.\n100\t- Phase 1 `Lorentz` is passive-only (`delta_eps >= 0`, `damping >= 0`, `frequency^2 != damping^2`), `Drude` is limited to damped poles (`damping > 0`) so it can lower cleanly onto the current pole-residue path, and `Debye` is passive-only (`delta_eps >= 0`, `tau > 0`).\n101\t- The current exact-hold pole-residue recurrence path can overflow for sufficiently stiff poles relative to `dt`; material compilation now raises an explicit error instead of emitting unusable coefficients, which is especially relevant for very small-`tau` Debye fits.\n102\t- Phase 1 anisotropy stops at diagonal tensor media: `AnisotropicMedium` is implemented and IR-lowered, `FullyAnisotropicMedium` remains an explicit deferred error for runtime compilation, and `Medium2D` remains a deferred direct-simulation feature with offline conversion helpers only.\n103\t- Advanced material policy is explicit rather than silent: `LossyMetalMedium` and perturbation media are parseable defer buckets with manual fallback helpers, while sampled `Custom*` media are parsed or bucketed only to produce clear reject errors.\n104\t- Phase 1 boundary runtime support now covers `Periodic`, `BlochBoundary`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, and baseline `PML`; `StablePML`, `Absorber`, and ABC-family entries remain outside the runtime compiler until later boundary tasks land.\n105\t- Conductor boundaries are represented as reflection-sign transforms rather than special-casing them inside the future timestep loop: PEC uses odd tangential electric and odd normal magnetic parity, while PMC uses the opposite parity assignment.\n106\t- Keep Phase 1 PML deliberately narrow: use Tidy3D-shaped `num_layers + parameters + extrude_structures` public models, compile one profile per boundary face, and treat the first runnable backend as a staged damping layer with persistent face memory rather than a full split-field CPML implementation.\n107\t- Keep `StablePML` as a preset-driven extension of the baseline staged PML backend in Phase 1 rather than a separate field-splitting formulation; runtime differentiation is through compiled profile metadata and defaults, not a distinct kernel family.\n108\t- Keep `Absorber` practical and explicit: Phase 1 uses a conductivity-ramp absorbing subset with no auxiliary memory contribution and a PEC-like outer-wall reflection policy, trading fidelity for a clear stable fallback when baseline PML is unsuitable.\n109\t- Keep `ABCBoundary` practical and narrow: Phase 1 implements only the explicit-parameter first-order subset, and automatic medium inference plus mode-based ABC stay deferred until scene-material sampling and mode-solver tasks are available.\n110\t- Keep runtime convergence explicit and narrow: Phase 1 shutoff is the Tidy3D-style ratio of instantaneous integrated electric-field intensity to its historical maximum, checked on a configurable step interval; material-aware CFL relaxation and richer convergence heuristics remain deferred.\n111\t- Keep runtime logging separate from benchmark metrics: Phase 1 emits deterministic progress records at step 0, configurable progress intervals, and final stop, while every shutoff check still lands in convergence evidence even when progress logging is downsampled.\n112\t- Keep Phase 1 source-time scope narrow and explicit: implement `GaussianPulse` and `ContinuousWave` as standalone immutable models first, preserve Tidy3D-style `freq0`/`fwidth`/`offset` semantics plus `remove_dc_component` on Gaussian pulses, and defer broader source-time families to their own tasks instead of hiding them behind loose dict payloads.\n113\t- Phase 1 `BroadbandPulse` is a clearly delimited analytic subset: represent it as a Gaussian-modulated carrier derived from `(freq_range, minimum_amplitude)` rather than trying to reproduce Tidy3D's licensed extension internals, and keep `CustomSourceTime` as explicit sample interpolation with nearest-value clamping outside the provided range.\n114\t- Phase 1 `UniformCurrentSource` uses primal-cell placement metadata rather than hidden source snapping: zero-size axes interpolate between neighboring cell centers when enabled, finite extents either select center-in-bounds cells or overlap-fraction weights when `confine_to_bounds=True`, and `\"total\"` amplitude normalization divides by the local transverse extent implied by the source geometry and resolved grid.\n115\t\n116\t## Task Handoffs\n117\t\n118\t- `task-003` should add versioned IR models under `autofdtd.ir` without coupling them to the public API module layout; it can treat `Simulation.to_payload()` as an interim handoff format, not the final IR contract.\n119\t- Later geometry, material, source, and monitor tasks can plug concrete tagged models into the existing container fields without changing the ordered-container semantics.\n120\t- `task-004` and `task-005` can reuse `ComponentIR.payload` plus the shared IR schema version constants instead of inventing another transport shape.\n121\t- Later geometry, source, monitor, boundary, and grid tasks should route new object models through `autofdtd.core.validation.normalize_component()` or extend its equivalents instead of scattering policy checks across each family.\n122\t- Later compiler work can rely on wrapper IR bounds as conservative planning envelopes; exact voxelization or boolean rasterization still belongs in scene compilation rather than the public geometry layer.\n123\t- Later compiler work can consume `StructureIR.source_index` plus `StructureIR.precedence_rank` directly when building ordered region materialization plans or conflict diagnostics.\n124\t- Later grid/compiler tasks should treat `LayerRefinementSpecIR` as planning metadata only; corner finding, snapping-point localization, and structure-derived meshing still need a separate scene-aware meshing pass.\n125\t- Material and compiler tasks should consume `SubpixelSpecIR.averaging_targets` and `requires_anisotropic_materialization` directly instead of re-deriving which interface families need tensor-valued effective media.\n126\t- Later runtime and chunk-planning tasks can treat `IsotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus `ConstitutiveMode` as the stable baseline constitutive contract before dispersive and anisotropic state is added.\n127\t- Later Lorentz, Drude, Debye, and Sellmeier tasks should lower onto the `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` auxiliary-state contract whenever the family can be reduced to pole-residue terms instead of creating parallel runtime state layouts.\n128\t- Later subpixel materialization work can target `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus the diagonal anisotropic kernel path instead of inventing a separate tensor-material runtime for effective-media smoothing.\n129\t- Later absorbing-boundary tasks should extend `CompiledBoundarySpec` and the boundary ghost-update stage rather than reworking the public `Boundary` or `BoundarySpec` container semantics; Bloch, symmetry, and baseline PML metadata now already live on that runtime contract.\n130\t- `task-022` should either preserve the `PMLParams` coefficient-shape contract for `StablePML` / `Absorber` or add a clearly separate compiled-face payload; `allocate_pml_boundary_state()` and `apply_boundary_stages()` are the current extension seams.\n131\t- Later ABC-boundary work should treat the current `pml_faces` metadata on `CompiledBoundarySpec` as the legacy absorbing-face alias and prefer the newer `stable_pml_faces` / `absorber_faces` tags when family-specific behavior matters.\n132\t- Later mode-ABC work should extend the new `abc_faces` / staged-ABC seam rather than folding mode-aware behavior into the generic halo transform path; the current deferral is specifically about mode-solver dependency, not about the ABC stage layout itself.\n133\t- Later execution-loop tasks should treat `CompiledRuntimeControls` and `RuntimeController` as the stable seam for stop reasons, early-stop evidence, and step-budget bookkeeping rather than burying convergence logic inside kernels or chunk schedulers.\n134\t- Later execution-loop tasks should route full Maxwell stepping through `RuntimeProgressLogger` / `RuntimeExecutionLog` instead of inventing a second progress format; benchmark tasks can layer throughput and JIT timing on top of the existing log bundle rather than mixing those concerns into debugging output.\n135\t- Later source-family tasks should reuse `source_time_model_from_value()` and `source_time_to_ir()` as the normalization and lowering seam for nested `source_time` fields instead of reimplementing waveform parsing inside each source model.\n136\t- Later source-time expansions should preserve the current JSON-safe complex-pair representation for custom envelopes so package serialization and IR transport stay stable even if dataset-backed APIs are added later.\n137\t- Later `PointDipole` and `CustomCurrentSource` work should reuse `CompiledAxisPlacement` / `CompiledUniformCurrentSource` conventions where possible, extending them for Yee-staggered component placement or dataset-driven amplitudes instead of inventing a separate source-placement runtime contract.\n138\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f649c44-f5ed-43a2-9485-dc3f20705005","uuid":"1ca11aa6-560e-4fe7-9088-f9147e5a4e26","timestamp":"2026-04-06T23:43:48.528Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/autofdtd/phase1/working-memory.md","content":"# Phase 1 Maxwell Solver Loop Working Memory\n\nUse this file for cumulative findings, reusable facts, decisions, and cross-task notes.\n\nKeep it concise. Do not repeat information already captured in `loop.prompt.md` or other local files.\nPrefer referencing local file paths and distilled takeaways instead of copying long excerpts.\n\nSuggested sections:\n\n- Facts\n- Decisions\n- Open Questions\n- Reusable References\n- Task Handoffs\n\n## Facts\n\n- `task-001` starts from an effectively empty repo with no `src/` package or tooling.\n- `phase1/feature-checklist.md` is now mirrored in code via `src/autofdtd/planning.py` so later tasks can reuse a local feature/status source instead of reparsing markdown.\n- `src/autofdtd/core/containers.py` now holds the first frozen tagged container models for `Structure`, `Scene`, and `Simulation`; `src/autofdtd/api/simulation.py` re-exports them as the public API surface.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now defines the first versioned execution IR, with `ComponentIR`, `StructureIR`, `SceneIR`, `SimulationIR`, and `ExecutionPackageIR` as the transport contract.\n- `src/autofdtd/core/validation.py` now centralizes Phase 1 normalization rules, unsupported-feature checks, primitive geometry sanity checks, simulation-bound checks, and zero-dimension boundary coercion.\n- `src/autofdtd/core/models.py` now provides the shared frozen model contract with `model_tag()`, `copy_update()`, `json_ready()`, and stable JSON serialization helpers reused by both public containers and IR models.\n- `src/autofdtd/geometry/primitives.py` now provides typed `Box`, `Sphere`, and `Cylinder` public models plus `GeometryTransform`, bounds, point-containment, and translation helpers.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers primitive geometries into typed `BoxIR`, `SphereIR`, and `CylinderIR` payloads with explicit bounds and scene-compilation transform metadata.\n- `src/autofdtd/geometry/polyslab.py` now provides a typed `PolySlab` model for simple-polygon extrusions with bounds, containment, translation, and simple-polygon validation; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers it into `PolySlabIR`.\n- `src/autofdtd/geometry/composite.py` now provides `GeometryGroup`, `Transformed`, `GeometryArray`, and a narrow `ClipOperation` subset (`union`, `intersection`, `difference`) for Phase 1 scene construction.\n- `GeometryTransform` now supports explicit rigid-transform composition plus translation, rotation, reflection, and conservative transformed-bounds evaluation for wrapper lowering and scene queries.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now carries typed wrapper IR via `GeometryGroupIR`, `TransformedIR`, and `ClipOperationIR`.\n- `GeometryArray` uses Tidy3D-style `geometry + offsets + transforms` public semantics, but Phase 1 keeps transforms rigid and linear-only and lowers arrays into explicit grouped instances for IR and overlap evaluation.\n- `Scene.structure_precedence()` and `Scene.resolve_structure_stack_at_point()` now expose concrete overlap order with `source_index`, `resolved_priority`, and `precedence_rank`, and `StructureIR` carries the same metadata for later compiler stages.\n- `src/autofdtd/grid/specs.py` now provides `AutoGrid`, `GridRefinement`, and `LayerRefinementSpec` in addition to the manual grid models; the current AutoGrid path is wavelength-driven, axis-local, and only applies axis-aligned layer refinement metadata.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `GridSpec` into typed grid IR with per-axis spec payloads, layer-refinement metadata, and resolved boundary and shape metadata, so later compiler work can consume inspectable discretization state instead of reparsing public models.\n- `src/autofdtd/grid/subpixel.py` now provides the first explicit subpixel policy layer with `SubpixelSpec`, `Staircasing`, and `PolarizedAveraging`, plus Tidy3D-style `bool` coercion for `Simulation.subpixel`.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers subpixel settings into `SubpixelSpecIR` with explicit averaging-vs-staircasing targets and an anisotropic-materialization flag for later scene compilation.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/isotropic.py` now provides typed `Medium`, `PECMedium`, and `PMCMedium` public models with validation and normalization helpers; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers them into typed `MediumIR`, `PECMediumIR`, and `PMCMediumIR`.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles isotropic constitutive coefficients, exposes scene-point medium sampling, and keeps explicit clamp-zero modes for `PECMedium` and `PMCMedium`.\n- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now provides the first staged constitutive-update helpers with a NumPy path and optional Warp-availability reporting.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now provides a Phase 1 `PoleResidue` public model with JSON-safe complex-pair storage, causal validation (`Re(a) <= 0`), `eps_model()`, and limited `Medium` conversion helpers.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now also provides a passive homogeneous `Sellmeier` model with `(B_i, C_i)` coefficient storage, direct `eps_model()` evaluation, single-pole `from_dispersion()` fitting, and explicit lowering to an equivalent `PoleResidue` representation.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/dispersive.py` now also provides passive homogeneous `Lorentz`, damped `Drude`, and passive homogeneous `Debye` models with family-specific analytical coefficients and explicit lowering to equivalent `PoleResidue` payloads.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers supported dispersive media into typed `PoleResidueIR` payloads instead of a generic medium envelope.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Sellmeier` into a typed `SellmeierIR` while keeping runtime compilation on the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` into typed IR families while runtime compilation still targets the shared pole-residue auxiliary-state contract.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `PoleResidue` media into explicit epsilon-infinity electric coefficients plus per-pole exact-hold recurrence terms for complex auxiliary polarization state.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Sellmeier` by first reducing it to an equivalent `PoleResidue`, then re-tagging the compiled coefficients as `medium_type=\"Sellmeier\"` so scene diagnostics preserve the public family name.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `Lorentz`, `Drude`, and `Debye` by first reducing them to equivalent `PoleResidue` terms, re-tagging the compiled coefficients with the public family name, and failing explicitly when converted poles are too stiff for the current exact-hold timestep path.\n- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now allocates explicit PoleResidue auxiliary state and applies a staged `pole_residue_electric_update()` that subtracts a compiled polarization-current estimate from the baseline electric update.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/anisotropic.py` now provides `AnisotropicMedium` as the implemented diagonal tensor family plus explicit `FullyAnisotropicMedium` and `Medium2D` defer-policy surfaces; `Medium2D` is parseable and convertible to volumetric `AnisotropicMedium`, but direct simulation use stays rejected in Phase 1.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/materials.py` now compiles `AnisotropicMedium` into `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` with one scalar constitutive branch per principal axis, allowing nondispersive, dispersive, and clamp-mode components to coexist without introducing a dense-tensor runtime yet.\n- `src/autofdtd/kernels/materials.py` now provides `allocate_anisotropic_state()`, `anisotropic_electric_update()`, and `anisotropic_magnetic_update()` for diagonal tensor updates with per-axis dispersive auxiliary state.\n- `src/autofdtd/materials/advanced.py` now maps `LossyMetalMedium`, `PerturbationMedium`, `PerturbationPoleResidue`, `CustomMedium`, and `CustomAnisotropicMedium` into explicit Phase 1 policy surfaces, and `src/autofdtd/core/validation.py` buckets unknown `Custom*` medium tags into the clear custom-media reject path instead of treating them as unmapped types.\n- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now provides the first typed non-absorbing boundary surface with `Periodic`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, `Boundary`, and `BoundarySpec`, including periodic-opposite-conductor coercion.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers the supported boundary subset into typed `PeriodicIR`, `PECBoundaryIR`, `PMCBoundaryIR`, `BoundaryIR`, and `BoundarySpecIR` payloads while leaving unsupported boundary families in the generic boundary envelope.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile the supported subset into explicit halo metadata and apply periodic-copy or conductor-reflection ghost-cell updates for `(nx, ny, nz, 3)` vector fields.\n- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now implements `BlochBoundary` plus `Boundary.bloch(...)`; runtime compilation lowers Bloch axes into explicit conjugate phase factors on `minus` faces and forward phase factors on `plus` faces, which forces complex field storage even when `bloch_vec == 0`.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py` and `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now attach symmetry-axis metadata as explicit electric and magnetic sign transforms derived from mirror parity, so chunk planning and future reduced-domain exchange can reuse the same compiled transform contract as conductor reflections.\n- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now implements baseline `PML` and `PMLParams`; `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` lowers them into typed `PMLIR` and `PMLParamsIR`, and `BoundarySpecIR` now records explicit `pml_axes` and `pml_faces`.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now prepare polynomial PML coefficients (`sigma`, `kappa`, `alpha`, attenuation and memory terms), allocate per-face persistent boundary-layer state, and run explicit `electric_pml` / `magnetic_pml` stages after halo handling.\n- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now also implements `AbsorberParams`, `StablePML`, and `Absorber`, with Tidy3D-aligned default presets, explicit constructors on `Boundary` / `BoundarySpec`, and zero-dimension coercion that treats all absorbing families consistently.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py` now compiles `StablePML` onto the existing staged PML coefficient path with a separate profile tag and compiles `Absorber` into conductivity-only attenuation coefficients plus a reflective outer-wall policy; `CompiledBoundarySpec` now records `stable_pml_*` and `absorber_*` face metadata in addition to the existing absorbing-face alias.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `StablePML` and `Absorber` into typed `StablePMLIR` and `AbsorberIR` payloads, with `AbsorberParamsIR` for conductivity-only profile metadata.\n- `src/autofdtd/boundaries/models.py` now adds `ABCBoundary` as an implemented first-order subset with explicit effective-medium inputs, plus parseable deferred `ModeABCBoundary`, `BroadbandModeABCSpec`, and `BroadbandModeABCFitterParam` compatibility surfaces.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/boundaries.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/boundaries.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/boundaries.py` now compile `ABCBoundary` into explicit first-order recurrence coefficients plus persistent per-face ABC state, while `ModeABCBoundary` fails with a clear runtime-deferred error until mode-solver work lands.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `ABCBoundary` into typed `ABCBoundaryIR` and records `abc_axes` / `abc_faces` on `BoundarySpecIR`; `ModeABCBoundary` remains a deferred public-model surface rather than a validated simulation feature.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/runtime.py` now compiles `Simulation` runtime controls into explicit `CompiledRuntimeControls` metadata with CFL-derived `dt`, Tidy3D-style `num_time_steps`, optional step-count override, shutoff policy, and carried `normalize_index`; `src/autofdtd/runtime/controls.py` evaluates those policies against integrated electric-field intensity without entangling them with the future Maxwell stepper.\n- `src/autofdtd/diagnostics/runtime_logging.py` now provides `RuntimeProgressLogger`, immutable `RuntimeLogEvent` / `RuntimeExecutionLog` records, and explicit `RuntimeConvergenceEvidence` capture; `src/autofdtd/runtime/controls.py` now exposes `run_until_stop_with_logging()` as the logged execution seam for precomputed intensity histories.\n- `src/autofdtd/sources/time.py` now provides Phase 1 `GaussianPulse` and `ContinuousWave` source-time profiles with finite parameter validation, analytic waveform evaluation, frequency-range helpers, and JSON-stable serialization through the shared tagged-model base.\n- `src/autofdtd/sources/time.py` now also implements a supported `BroadbandPulse` Gaussian-subset wrapper and an interpolated `CustomSourceTime` with explicit `(time_samples, envelope_values)` storage using JSON-safe complex pairs.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers source-time profiles into typed `GaussianPulseIR` and `ContinuousWaveIR` payloads, and generic source component envelopes serialize nested `source_time` entries as typed IR dicts rather than opaque model payloads.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now also lowers `BroadbandPulse` and `CustomSourceTime` into typed IR payloads carrying derived timing/frequency metadata plus serialized custom-envelope samples.\n- `src/autofdtd/sources/current.py` now implements `UniformCurrentSource` with Tidy3D-aligned `center`, `size`, `polarization`, `source_time`, `interpolate`, `confine_to_bounds`, and `current_amplitude_definition` fields plus explicit `field_kind`, `component_axis`, `zero_size_axes`, `placement_kind`, and `support_bounds` semantics.\n- `src/autofdtd/compiler/sources.py`, `src/autofdtd/kernels/sources.py`, and `src/autofdtd/runtime/sources.py` now compile uniform current sources onto resolved primal-cell grids using overlap-or-interpolation axis placement, total-current normalization by transverse extent, and staged NumPy injection helpers for `(nx, ny, nz, 3)` electric or magnetic field buffers.\n- `src/autofdtd/ir/models.py` now lowers `UniformCurrentSource` into typed `UniformCurrentSourceIR` payloads instead of only the generic source envelope, and `src/autofdtd/examples/current_sources.py` plus `tests/test_sources_current.py` exercise placement, IR lowering, compilation, and runtime injection end to end.\n\n## Decisions\n\n- Reserve subsystem namespaces now: `api`, `core`, `geometry`, `materials`, `boundaries`, `sources`, `monitors`, `grid`, `modes`, `ir`, `compiler`, `runtime`, `kernels`, `diagnostics`, `examples`.\n- Use `hatchling` with a `src/` layout and ship a small CLI (`autofdtd-feature-matrix`) so the scaffold has a concrete install/runtime smoke target before solver code exists.\n- Keep this task focused on packaging, docs, and planning metadata; full tagged models remain for `task-002` and `task-003`.\n- Model ordered overlap explicitly through `Scene.structures_in_resolution_order()`, sorting by resolved priority and then insertion order so later structures still win ties during sequential materialization.\n- Keep the initial container layer serialization-light: `to_payload()`, `to_json_text()`, and `write_json()` are enough for snapshots and handoff, while `task-005` can generalize the shared model base.\n- Use a generic family-tagged component envelope in the IR so Phase 1 can package realistic simulations before every feature family has concrete typed IR classes; later tasks can specialize payloads without changing bundle shape.\n- Bundle packaging is directory-based: `manifest.json` points at a hashed `simulation.json` entrypoint, avoiding self-referential manifest hashing while staying suitable for remote upload or local caching.\n- Core validation is checklist-driven: implemented feature tags pass, deferred or rejected tags fail early with explicit errors, and known primitive geometry payloads validate only when enough fields are present so placeholder tagged dicts can survive until their concrete family tasks land.\n- Shared model behavior should live in `autofdtd.core.models`; public API and IR classes should subclass it rather than duplicating serialization or copy helpers in subsystem-local base classes.\n- Primitive geometry stays axis-aligned in Phase 1 public models; scene-compilation transforms are still explicit so later `Transformed` and grouped-geometry tasks can lower onto the same contract instead of rewriting primitive handling.\n- Structure precedence is now inspectable with `Scene.resolve_structure_at_point()`, which mirrors materialization order by applying primitive containment checks in resolved-priority order.\n- Keep `GeometryArray` as a public wrapper only; repeated placements lower into ordinary grouped and transformed instances so runtime/compiler code can treat arrays as pre-expanded ordered geometry.\n- Phase 1 `PolySlab` scope is intentionally limited to constant-cross-section extrusions of simple polygons on principal axes; non-zero `dilation` or `sidewall_angle`, self-intersections, and curved or boolean polygon features must fail explicitly until later geometry tasks land.\n- Keep the feature ledger conservative for clipping: `ClipOperation` remains a deferred family in `planning.py`, but geometry validation explicitly allows the implemented subset and rejects unsupported boolean ops with clear errors.\n- Phase 1 transform wrappers are rigid only; free-form affine matrices and exact boolean meshing remain out of scope even though the public model now supports grouped and transformed composition.\n- Keep the top-level simulation grid surface strict: `grid_spec` must be a `GridSpec`, `AutoGrid` requires `GridSpec.wavelength`, and `QuasiUniformGrid` remains an explicit deferred error.\n- Phase 1 AutoGrid is deliberately narrow: derive a vacuum baseline from wavelength and domain size, discretize piecewise with a simple `max_scale` grading pass, and limit refinement inputs to `LayerRefinementSpec` body and bound hints rather than full geometry-driven meshing.\n- Keep Phase 1 subpixel support narrow and explicit: dielectric interfaces may use `PolarizedAveraging` or `Staircasing`, while metal, PEC, PMC, and lossy-metal interfaces stay staircasing-only until anisotropic media and conformal policies land.\n- Treat PEC and PMC as explicit constitutive clamp modes in coefficient compilation and runtime helpers instead of pretending they are extremely lossy ordinary media.\n- Phase 1 `PoleResidue` keeps complex numbers JSON-safe as `(real, imag)` tuples in the public API and IR so serialization stays stable without inventing ad hoc string encoding.\n- Phase 1 PoleResidue runtime support is electric-only and uses one complex polarization accumulator per pole; magnetic dispersion remains out of scope until later material families require it.\n- Phase 1 `Sellmeier` support is intentionally passive-only and homogeneous-only: `B_i >= 0`, `C_i >= 0`, zero-`C_i` terms fold into `eps_inf`, and all timestep execution reuses the existing electric-only `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` path instead of creating a new auxiliary-state layout.\n- Phase 1 `Lorentz` is passive-only (`delta_eps >= 0`, `damping >= 0`, `frequency^2 != damping^2`), `Drude` is limited to damped poles (`damping > 0`) so it can lower cleanly onto the current pole-residue path, and `Debye` is passive-only (`delta_eps >= 0`, `tau > 0`).\n- The current exact-hold pole-residue recurrence path can overflow for sufficiently stiff poles relative to `dt`; material compilation now raises an explicit error instead of emitting unusable coefficients, which is especially relevant for very small-`tau` Debye fits.\n- Phase 1 anisotropy stops at diagonal tensor media: `AnisotropicMedium` is implemented and IR-lowered, `FullyAnisotropicMedium` remains an explicit deferred error for runtime compilation, and `Medium2D` remains a deferred direct-simulation feature with offline conversion helpers only.\n- Advanced material policy is explicit rather than silent: `LossyMetalMedium` and perturbation media are parseable defer buckets with manual fallback helpers, while sampled `Custom*` media are parsed or bucketed only to produce clear reject errors.\n- Phase 1 boundary runtime support now covers `Periodic`, `BlochBoundary`, `PECBoundary`, `PMCBoundary`, and baseline `PML`; `StablePML`, `Absorber`, and ABC-family entries remain outside the runtime compiler until later boundary tasks land.\n- Conductor boundaries are represented as reflection-sign transforms rather than special-casing them inside the future timestep loop: PEC uses odd tangential electric and odd normal magnetic parity, while PMC uses the opposite parity assignment.\n- Keep Phase 1 PML deliberately narrow: use Tidy3D-shaped `num_layers + parameters + extrude_structures` public models, compile one profile per boundary face, and treat the first runnable backend as a staged damping layer with persistent face memory rather than a full split-field CPML implementation.\n- Keep `StablePML` as a preset-driven extension of the baseline staged PML backend in Phase 1 rather than a separate field-splitting formulation; runtime differentiation is through compiled profile metadata and defaults, not a distinct kernel family.\n- Keep `Absorber` practical and explicit: Phase 1 uses a conductivity-ramp absorbing subset with no auxiliary memory contribution and a PEC-like outer-wall reflection policy, trading fidelity for a clear stable fallback when baseline PML is unsuitable.\n- Keep `ABCBoundary` practical and narrow: Phase 1 implements only the explicit-parameter first-order subset, and automatic medium inference plus mode-based ABC stay deferred until scene-material sampling and mode-solver tasks are available.\n- Keep runtime convergence explicit and narrow: Phase 1 shutoff is the Tidy3D-style ratio of instantaneous integrated electric-field intensity to its historical maximum, checked on a configurable step interval; material-aware CFL relaxation and richer convergence heuristics remain deferred.\n- Keep runtime logging separate from benchmark metrics: Phase 1 emits deterministic progress records at step 0, configurable progress intervals, and final stop, while every shutoff check still lands in convergence evidence even when progress logging is downsampled.\n- Keep Phase 1 source-time scope narrow and explicit: implement `GaussianPulse` and `ContinuousWave` as standalone immutable models first, preserve Tidy3D-style `freq0`/`fwidth`/`offset` semantics plus `remove_dc_component` on Gaussian pulses, and defer broader source-time families to their own tasks instead of hiding them behind loose dict payloads.\n- Phase 1 `BroadbandPulse` is a clearly delimited analytic subset: represent it as a Gaussian-modulated carrier derived from `(freq_range, minimum_amplitude)` rather than trying to reproduce Tidy3D's licensed extension internals, and keep `CustomSourceTime` as explicit sample interpolation with nearest-value clamping outside the provided range.\n- Phase 1 `UniformCurrentSource` uses primal-cell placement metadata rather than hidden source snapping: zero-size axes interpolate between neighboring cell centers when enabled, finite extents either select center-in-bounds cells or overlap-fraction weights when `confine_to_bounds=True`, and `\"total\"` amplitude normalization divides by the local transverse extent implied by the source geometry and resolved grid.\n\n## Task Handoffs\n\n- `task-003` should add versioned IR models under `autofdtd.ir` without coupling them to the public API module layout; it can treat `Simulation.to_payload()` as an interim handoff format, not the final IR contract.\n- Later geometry, material, source, and monitor tasks can plug concrete tagged models into the existing container fields without changing the ordered-container semantics.\n- `task-004` and `task-005` can reuse `ComponentIR.payload` plus the shared IR schema version constants instead of inventing another transport shape.\n- Later geometry, source, monitor, boundary, and grid tasks should route new object models through `autofdtd.core.validation.normalize_component()` or extend its equivalents instead of scattering policy checks across each family.\n- Later compiler work can rely on wrapper IR bounds as conservative planning envelopes; exact voxelization or boolean rasterization still belongs in scene compilation rather than the public geometry layer.\n- Later compiler work can consume `StructureIR.source_index` plus `StructureIR.precedence_rank` directly when building ordered region materialization plans or conflict diagnostics.\n- Later grid/compiler tasks should treat `LayerRefinementSpecIR` as planning metadata only; corner finding, snapping-point localization, and structure-derived meshing still need a separate scene-aware meshing pass.\n- Material and compiler tasks should consume `SubpixelSpecIR.averaging_targets` and `requires_anisotropic_materialization` directly instead of re-deriving which interface families need tensor-valued effective media.\n- Later runtime and chunk-planning tasks can treat `IsotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus `ConstitutiveMode` as the stable baseline constitutive contract before dispersive and anisotropic state is added.\n- Later Lorentz, Drude, Debye, and Sellmeier tasks should lower onto the `PoleResidueMaterialCoefficients` auxiliary-state contract whenever the family can be reduced to pole-residue terms instead of creating parallel runtime state layouts.\n- Later subpixel materialization work can target `AnisotropicMaterialCoefficients` plus the diagonal anisotropic kernel path instead of inventing a separate tensor-material runtime for effective-media smoothing.\n- Later absorbing-boundary tasks should extend `CompiledBoundarySpec` and the boundary ghost-update stage rather than reworking the public `Boundary` or `BoundarySpec` container semantics; Bloch, symmetry, and baseline PML metadata now already live on that runtime contract.\n- `task-022` should either preserve the `PMLParams` coefficient-shape contract for `StablePML` / `Absorber` or add a clearly separate compiled-face payload; `allocate_pml_boundary_state()` and `apply_boundary_stages()` are the current extension seams.\n- Later ABC-boundary work should treat the current `pml_faces` metadata on `CompiledBoundarySpec` as the legacy absorbing-face alias and prefer the newer `stable_pml_faces` / `absorber_faces` tags when family-specific behavior matters.\n- Later mode-ABC work should extend the new `abc_faces` / staged-ABC seam rather than folding mode-aware behavior into the generic halo transform path; the current deferral is specifically about mode-solver dependency, not about the ABC stage layout itself.\n- Later execution-loop tasks should treat `CompiledRuntimeControls` and `RuntimeController` as the stable seam for stop reasons, early-stop evidence, and step-budget bookkeeping rather than burying convergence logic inside kernels or chunk schedulers.\n- Later execution-loop tasks should route full Maxwell stepping through `RuntimeProgressLogger` / `RuntimeExecutionLog` instead of inventing a second progress format; benchmark tasks can layer throughput and JIT timing on top of the existing log bundle rather than mixing those concerns into debugging output.\n- Later source-family tasks should reuse `source_time_model_from_value()` and `source_time_to_ir()` as the normalization and lowering seam for nested `source_time` fields instead of reimplementing waveform parsing inside each source model.\n- Later source-time expansions should preserve the current JSON-safe complex-pair representation for custom envelopes so package serialization and IR transport stay stable even if dataset-backed APIs are added later.\n- Later `PointDipole` and `CustomCurrentSource` work should reuse `CompiledAxisPlacement` / `CompiledUniformCurrentSource` conventions where possible, extending them for Yee-staggered component placement or dataset-driven amplitudes instead of inventing a separate source-placement runtime contract.\n","numLines":138,"startLine":1,"totalLines":138}}}
