\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"9f649c44-f5ed-43a2-9485-dc3f20705005\",\"uuid\":\"f6485b06-1b98-46f2-a203-4b6fbe22c5b8\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_a15fxht0aave_3\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"No files found\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"9f649c44-f5ed-43a2-9485-dc3f20705005\",\"uuid\":\"1ce7df82-9c33-4f4e-b7bb-c24061e6d47f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-06T23:43:47.232Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"filenames\":[],\"durationMs\":5,\"numFiles\":0,\"truncated\":false}}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_a15fxht0aave_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t# Phase 1 Maxwell Solver Loop\\n2\\t\\n3\\tDrive Phase 1 of autofdtd from an almost-empty repository to a credible, pip-installable, GPU-first FDTD package whose feature plan is anchored explicitly to the local Tidy3D EM API surface. The loop must produce a Tidy3D-inspired public API, a tagged execution IR, a scene-compilation pipeline, a chunk-based multi-node-ready runtime, Warp kernel conventions, concrete implementations for the highest-priority Tidy3D feature families, validation examples, and documentation without copying protected code or docs from any reference project.\\n4\\t\\n5\\tStatuses: `QUEUED`, `RUNNING`, `COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, `BLOCKED`\\n6\\t\\n7\\t## [COMPLETED] task-001 - Create the package, tooling, and docs scaffold around the planned feature surface\\n8\\tSuccess: Create a pip-installable src-layout package with module namespaces, development tooling, test entrypoints, and docs scaffolding that are aligned with the feature checklist and subsystem boundaries.\\n9\\tAttempts: 1\\n10\\tLast Run: task-001-attempt-001\\n11\\tLast Duration: 335.1s\\n12\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:09:22Z\\n13\\tNotes: The Tidy3D feature checklist is already part of prep and should be consumed here rather than regenerated.\\n14\\t\\n15\\t## [COMPLETED] task-002 - Implement the tagged core Simulation, Scene, and Structure data model\\n16\\tSuccess: Provide the foundational public and IR-facing container models for Simulation, Scene, and Structure, including ordered structure semantics, names, priority behavior, and serialization scaffolding.\\n17\\tAttempts: 1\\n18\\tLast Run: task-002-attempt-001\\n19\\tLast Duration: 318.3s\\n20\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:14:41Z\\n21\\tNotes: This is the container backbone that many later feature tasks depend on.\\n22\\t\\n23\\t## [COMPLETED] task-003 - Define the tagged execution IR and serialization contract\\n24\\tSuccess: Land an inspectable, versioned IR schema and serialization plan that can represent realistic Phase 1 simulations independently of the public API and is suitable for local or remote execution packaging.\\n25\\tAttempts: 1\\n26\\tLast Run: task-003-attempt-001\\n27\\tLast Duration: 249.2s\\n28\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:18:50Z\\n29\\tNotes: The IR should be specific enough to support later feature tasks without redesign.\\n30\\t\\n31\\t## [COMPLETED] task-004 - Implement core normalization and validation behavior\\n32\\tSuccess: Provide validation and normalization passes for names, bounds, basic shape constraints, unsupported-feature errors, and selected warn-and-coerce behaviors that align with the feature checklist.\\n33\\tAttempts: 1\\n34\\tLast Run: task-004-attempt-001\\n35\\tLast Duration: 499.7s\\n36\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:27:10Z\\n37\\tNotes: This task should make later feature work safer instead of treating validation as cleanup at the end.\\n38\\t\\n39\\t## [COMPLETED] task-005 - Implement shared model-base and serialization helpers\\n40\\tSuccess: Provide the shared base-model, tagging, copy-or-update, and serialization helpers needed by the public API and IR layers so later feature tasks build on a common contract.\\n41\\tAttempts: 1\\n42\\tLast Run: task-005-attempt-001\\n43\\tLast Duration: 164.9s\\n44\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:29:54Z\\n45\\tNotes: This keeps tagging and serialization from being reimplemented ad hoc in every feature family.\\n46\\t\\n47\\t## [COMPLETED] task-006 - Implement primitive geometry support for Box, Sphere, and Cylinder\\n48\\tSuccess: Provide public and IR representations plus tests for Box, Sphere, and Cylinder geometries, including bounds, transforms needed for scene compilation, and precedence interaction inside structures.\\n49\\tAttempts: 1\\n50\\tLast Run: task-006-attempt-001\\n51\\tLast Duration: 406.1s\\n52\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:36:41Z\\n53\\tNotes: Start with the simplest Tidy3D geometry primitives first.\\n54\\t\\n55\\t## [COMPLETED] task-007 - Implement PolySlab and polygon-driven geometry support\\n56\\tSuccess: Support PolySlab-style polygon extrusion geometry with explicit scope and limitation documentation, plus tests that cover representative planar photonics cases.\\n57\\tAttempts: 1\\n58\\tLast Run: task-007-attempt-001\\n59\\tLast Duration: 460.5s\\n60\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:44:21Z\\n61\\tNotes: This is one of the most important geometry families for Tidy3D-style compatibility.\\n62\\t\\n63\\t## [COMPLETED] task-008 - Implement geometry grouping, transforms, and clip-style wrappers\\n64\\tSuccess: Provide GeometryGroup-style composition plus the minimum transform and clip operation support needed for credible Phase 1 scene construction.\\n65\\tAttempts: 1\\n66\\tLast Run: task-008-attempt-001\\n67\\tLast Duration: 531.5s\\n68\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:53:13Z\\n69\\tNotes: This task should turn individual primitives into useful scene-building blocks.\\n70\\t\\n71\\t## [COMPLETED] task-009 - Implement geometry arrays, precedence, and repeated placement semantics\\n72\\tSuccess: Support repeated or array-style geometry placement and explicit precedence semantics for ordered structures with tests that verify overlap behavior.\\n73\\tAttempts: 1\\n74\\tLast Run: task-009-attempt-001\\n75\\tLast Duration: 378.3s\\n76\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T02:59:31Z\\n77\\tNotes: This is where structure ordering becomes concrete.\\n78\\t\\n79\\t## [COMPLETED] task-010 - Implement grid controls for UniformGrid, CustomGrid, and GridSpec core paths\\n80\\tSuccess: Provide the core grid specification surface needed for Phase 1 simulations, including uniform grids, custom boundaries or custom grids, and GridSpec-style composition.\\n81\\tAttempts: 1\\n82\\tLast Run: task-010-attempt-001\\n83\\tLast Duration: 678.8s\\n84\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:10:50Z\\n85\\tNotes: Grid support should be concrete enough to drive later discretization and mode-solver work.\\n86\\t\\n87\\t## [COMPLETED] task-011 - Implement AutoGrid and refinement-spec planning surfaces\\n88\\tSuccess: Support an initial AutoGrid-style and refinement-spec surface with clear limitations and lowering behavior, even if the first meshing policy is simpler than Tidy3D's full behavior.\\n89\\tAttempts: 1\\n90\\tLast Run: task-011-attempt-001\\n91\\tLast Duration: 414.5s\\n92\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:17:44Z\\n93\\tNotes: This task can begin with planning or simplified refinement behavior, but it must not be hand-wavy.\\n94\\t\\n95\\t## [COMPLETED] task-012 - Implement SubpixelSpec and core averaging policy models\\n96\\tSuccess: Provide SubpixelSpec-style controls and the core averaging or staircasing policy representations needed to drive geometry materialization behavior.\\n97\\tAttempts: 1\\n98\\tLast Run: task-012-attempt-001\\n99\\tLast Duration: 629.8s\\n100\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:28:14Z\\n101\\tNotes: This task is about policy modeling before the heavier compiler behavior lands.\\n102\\t\\n103\\t## [COMPLETED] task-013 - Implement isotropic Medium, PECMedium, and PMCMedium with coefficient compilation and constitutive kernels\\n104\\tSuccess: Support the foundational isotropic material family, including plain Medium and perfect-conductor medium variants, with tests for serialization, validation, structure application, coefficient preparation, and the corresponding constitutive-update kernel paths.\\n105\\tAttempts: 1\\n106\\tLast Run: task-013-attempt-001\\n107\\tLast Duration: 589.0s\\n108\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:38:03Z\\n109\\tNotes: These are the baseline material types many later tasks assume exist.\\n110\\t\\n111\\t## [COMPLETED] task-014 - Implement PoleResidue medium support with coefficient prep and update kernels\\n112\\tSuccess: Support PoleResidue-style dispersive media with explicit API and IR models, coefficient preparation, auxiliary-state handling, update-kernel support, and representative tests.\\n113\\tAttempts: 1\\n114\\tLast Run: task-014-attempt-001\\n115\\tLast Duration: 522.2s\\n116\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:46:45Z\\n117\\tNotes: Keep PoleResidue separate because it is the most general dispersive family and often subsumes the others conceptually.\\n118\\t\\n119\\t## [COMPLETED] task-015 - Implement Sellmeier medium support with coefficient prep and update kernels\\n120\\tSuccess: Support Sellmeier media or a clearly documented supported subset, including API and IR models, coefficient preparation, update-kernel support, and representative validation tests.\\n121\\tAttempts: 1\\n122\\tLast Run: task-015-attempt-001\\n123\\tLast Duration: 299.6s\\n124\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:51:45Z\\n125\\tNotes: This stays separate so optical-material support is not hidden inside a large dispersive bucket.\\n126\\t\\n127\\t## [COMPLETED] task-016 - Implement Lorentz, Drude, and Debye medium support with update kernels\\n128\\tSuccess: Support Lorentz, Drude, and Debye medium families or a clearly delimited subset, including API and IR models, coefficient preparation, update-kernel support, and tests that show each family exercises the intended constitutive path.\\n129\\tAttempts: 1\\n130\\tLast Run: task-016-attempt-001\\n131\\tLast Duration: 485.0s\\n132\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T03:59:50Z\\n133\\tNotes: These families are close enough to share a task without becoming ambiguous if the tests name each family explicitly.\\n134\\t\\n135\\t## [COMPLETED] task-017 - Implement anisotropic medium families, Medium2D policy, and anisotropic kernel support\\n136\\tSuccess: Support the initial anisotropic medium surface, including a clear policy for AnisotropicMedium, FullyAnisotropicMedium, and Medium2D-related behavior, with explicit tests, limitations, coefficient preparation, and anisotropic kernel support.\\n137\\tAttempts: 19\\n138\\tLast Run: task-017-attempt-019\\n139\\tLast Duration: 502.8s\\n140\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:06:42Z\\n141\\tNotes: This task should respect the fact that subpixel smoothing can create effective anisotropy and must land before second-order subpixel materialization is treated as implemented.\\n142\\t\\n143\\t## [COMPLETED] task-018 - Implement perturbation, lossy-metal, and advanced-material defer buckets\\n144\\tSuccess: Provide an explicit Phase 1 handling policy for PerturbationMedium, LossyMetalMedium, custom media, and other advanced material families so the API surface is mapped rather than ignored.\\n145\\tAttempts: 1\\n146\\tLast Run: task-018-attempt-001\\n147\\tLast Duration: 399.5s\\n148\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:13:22Z\\n149\\tNotes: This is intentionally a policy-heavy task if full implementation is not yet realistic.\\n150\\t\\n151\\t## [COMPLETED] task-019 - Implement boundary support for Periodic, PECBoundary, PMCBoundary, and Boundary containers\\n152\\tSuccess: Support the foundational non-absorbing boundary surface, including Boundary and BoundarySpec composition, periodic behavior, conductor boundaries, and the runtime transform or kernel support needed by the timestep loop.\\n153\\tAttempts: 1\\n154\\tLast Run: task-019-attempt-001\\n155\\tLast Duration: 708.3s\\n156\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:25:10Z\\n157\\tNotes: These are the first boundary primitives the compiler and runtime should rely on.\\n158\\t\\n159\\t## [COMPLETED] task-020 - Implement BlochBoundary, symmetry-aware metadata, and phase-aware runtime transforms\\n160\\tSuccess: Provide BlochBoundary support and the related symmetry-aware metadata and runtime transform support needed for chunk planning and execution lowering.\\n161\\tAttempts: 1\\n162\\tLast Run: task-020-attempt-001\\n163\\tLast Duration: 460.9s\\n164\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:32:51Z\\n165\\tNotes: This task is important because periodic-phase behavior leaks into runtime metadata and not just public API models.\\n166\\t\\n167\\t## [COMPLETED] task-021 - Implement PML boundary support with coefficient prep and kernel stages\\n168\\tSuccess: Support PML boundaries with explicit API and IR models, coefficient preparation, boundary-layer state allocation, kernel-stage integration, and representative absorption tests.\\n169\\tAttempts: 1\\n170\\tLast Run: task-021-attempt-001\\n171\\tLast Duration: 632.6s\\n172\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:43:24Z\\n173\\tNotes: PML is important enough to deserve its own task instead of sharing a generic absorbing-boundary bucket.\\n174\\t\\n175\\t## [COMPLETED] task-022 - Implement StablePML and Absorber boundary support with coefficient prep and kernel stages\\n176\\tSuccess: Support StablePML and Absorber boundaries or a clearly delimited subset, including API and IR models, coefficient preparation, kernel-stage integration, and representative tests for practical absorbing-boundary behavior.\\n177\\tAttempts: 1\\n178\\tLast Run: task-022-attempt-001\\n179\\tLast Duration: 524.5s\\n180\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T19:52:08Z\\n181\\tNotes: StablePML and Absorber are separate enough from baseline PML to justify their own task.\\n182\\t\\n183\\t## [COMPLETED] task-023 - Implement ABCBoundary, ModeABCBoundary, and advanced boundary defer policy\\n184\\tSuccess: Provide an explicit Phase 1 treatment for ABCBoundary, ModeABCBoundary, and other specialized boundary variants, including concrete implementation where practical and clear reject-or-defer behavior otherwise.\\n185\\tAttempts: 1\\n186\\tLast Run: task-023-attempt-001\\n187\\tLast Duration: 823.0s\\n188\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T20:05:51Z\\n189\\tNotes: This task prevents specialized Tidy3D boundary features from disappearing into ambiguity.\\n190\\t\\n191\\t## [COMPLETED] task-024 - Implement Simulation runtime controls for timestep count, shutoff, and convergence policy\\n192\\tSuccess: Support the core Simulation runtime-control surface needed for practical runs, including run-time or step-count control, shutoff or early-stop criteria, and clearly defined convergence-related behavior.\\n193\\tAttempts: 1\\n194\\tLast Run: task-024-attempt-001\\n195\\tLast Duration: 696.8s\\n196\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T20:17:28Z\\n197\\tNotes: This task makes convergence and stop behavior explicit instead of leaving it buried inside the simulation loop.\\n198\\t\\n199\\t## [COMPLETED] task-025 - Implement detailed simulation logging, progress reporting, and convergence evidence capture\\n200\\tSuccess: Execution paths emit structured progress logs, stop-reason records, convergence or shutoff evidence, and enough runtime detail to diagnose practical simulation behavior during end-to-end runs.\\n201\\tAttempts: 1\\n202\\tLast Run: task-025-attempt-001\\n203\\tLast Duration: 341.6s\\n204\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T20:23:10Z\\n205\\tNotes: Keep this distinct from benchmark metrics so debugging and validation remain first-class.\\n206\\t\\n207\\t## [COMPLETED] task-026 - Implement GaussianPulse and ContinuousWave source-time profiles\\n208\\tSuccess: Support GaussianPulse and ContinuousWave source-time profiles with explicit parameter validation, serialization, IR lowering, and representative waveform tests.\\n209\\tAttempts: 1\\n210\\tLast Run: task-026-attempt-001\\n211\\tLast Duration: 752.9s\\n212\\tCompleted At: 2026-04-06T20:35:43Z\\n213\\tNotes: These are the two most common source-time profiles and should be available early.\\n214\\t\\n215\\t## [COMPLETED] task-027 - Implement 